### Step 1: Set your GitHub username

Copy and paste:

bash
```
git config --global user.name "Parthh-1621"
```

### Step 2: Set your email

Replace the email below with the email associated with your GitHub account.

bash
```
git config --global user.email "your-email@example.com"
```

### Step 3: Verify your details

bash
```
git config --global --get user.name
git config --global --get user.email
```

## Part 2: Push and compare branches

### Step 4: Fetch the latest changes

Make sure you are inside `~/StudentProject` and on the `feature` branch.

bash
```
git fetch origin
```

### Step 5: Push the feature branch

bash
```
git push -u origin feature
```

### Step 6: Compare `feature` with `main`

bash
```
git diff origin/main..origin/feature
```

To see a summary of the changes:

bash
```
git diff --stat origin/main..origin/feature
```

## Part 3: Create and merge the pull request

### Step 7: Open your repository

Go to [StudentProject on GitHub](<https://github.com/Parthh-1621/StudentProject>).

### Step 8: Create the pull request

1. Click Pull requests.
2. Click New pull request.
3. Set base to `main`.
4. Set compare to `feature`.
5. Click Create pull request.

### Step 9: Merge the pull request

1. Click Merge pull request.
2. Click Confirm merge.

## Part 4: Update your local main branch

### Step 10: Run these commands in Git Bash

bash
```
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git log -1 --oneline
```

Note: You do not need `gh pr create` because GitHub CLI is not installed on your computer. Creating and merging the pull request through the GitHub website works just as well.
